<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.5.1: Writing and Evaluating New Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00441</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.5.1: Writing and Evaluating New Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>eb4a873f-8416-4467-a507-cbb4625d1766</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Look at the following equation: \(50 + 1 = 51\).</para>
    <para>Perform each of the following operations and answer these questions: What does each resulting equation look like? Is it still a true equation?</para>
    <para>1. Add 12 to each side of the equation.</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para/>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(50 + 1 + 12 = 51 + 12\), or \(63 = 63\) (or equivalent). Yes, it is still a true equation. </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Question 2-->
<exercise>
  <exercise>
    <problem/><solution/></exercise>
<problem/><solution>
      
      <para>Enter your answers here:</para>
    <para>Compare your answer:</para>
      <para>\(50 + 1 + 10 + 2 = 51 + 12\), or \(63 = 63\) (or equivalent). Yes, it is still a true equation. </para>
    </solution></exercise>
<!--Question 3--><exercise>
  <exercise>
    <problem/><solution/></exercise>
<problem>
    <para><span/></para>
  </problem><solution>
      
      <para>Enter your answers here:</para>
    <para>Compare your answer:</para>
      <para>\(50 + 1 + 4 + 3 = 51 + 7\), or \(58 = 58\) (or equivalent). Yes, it is still a true equation. </para>
    </solution></exercise>
<!--Question 4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para><span/></para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answers here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
    <list>
      <item>\(8 + 2 = 10\). Added to the original equation gives: \(50 + 1 + 8 + 2 = 51 + 10\), or \(51 + 10 = 61\), or \(61 = 61\).</item>
      <item>\(a + a = 2a\). Added to the original equation gives: \(50 + 1 + a + a = 51 + 2a\), or \(51 + 2a = 51 + 2a\).</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Question 5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para><span/></para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answers here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples</para>
    <list>
      <item>\(3 + 2 = 7\). Added to the original equation gives: \(50 + 1 + 3 + 2 = 51 + 7\), or \(56 = 58\), which is false. </item>
      <item>\(a + 2 = a\). Added to the original equation gives: \(50 + 1 + a + 2 = 51 + a\), or \(53 + a = 51 + a\), which is false. </item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
</section>
